JOB 13 – A Bin of Parts
Title
Study of Lists and List Operations
A list is a collection of elements stored inside square brackets [ ]. Unlike strings, lists are mutable, which means their elements can be changed.
Example:
bin = [25.02, 24.98, 25.05]
1. Creating a List
dias = [25.02, 24.98, 25.05]

print(dias)
Output:
[25.02, 24.98, 25.05]
2. Accessing List Elements
print(dias[0])
print(dias[1])
Output:
25.02
24.98
Lists also use zero-based indexing.
3. Changing a List Element
Unlike strings, list elements can be changed.
bin = [25.02, 24.98, 25.05]

bin[0] = 25.01

print(bin)
Output:
[25.01, 24.98, 25.05]
4. append()
append() adds an element at the end of the list.
bin = [25.02, 24.98, 25.05]

bin.append(25.10)

print(bin)
Output:
[25.02, 24.98, 25.05, 25.10]
5. insert()
insert() adds an element at a specified position.
bin.insert(1, 24.90)

print(bin)
The new element is inserted at index 1.
6. pop()
pop() removes an element using its position and returns that element.
bin = [25.02, 24.98, 25.05]

x = bin.pop()

print(x)
print(bin)
It removes the last element by default.
A particular position can also be specified:
bin.pop(1)
7. remove()
remove() removes an element by its value.
bin = [25.02, 24.98, 25.05]

bin.remove(24.98)

print(bin)
Output:
[25.02, 25.05]
Difference
pop() → removes by position
remove() → removes by value
8. sort()
sort() arranges the list in ascending order.
dias = [25.05, 24.98, 25.02]

dias.sort()

print(dias)
Output:
[24.98, 25.02, 25.05]
9. reverse()
reverse() reverses the order of elements.
dias.reverse()

print(dias)
10. List Slicing
Lists also support slicing.
dias = [25.02, 24.98, 25.05, 24.90]

print(dias[1:3])
Output:
[24.98, 25.05]
